PostgreSQL 19rc1: btree_gist's varlena penalty compares at the first byte the bounds differ in (upstream b82d69abf6).

gbt_var_penalty prices adding a key to a subtree, when the key extends the subtree's bounds without shortening their
common prefix, by the bytes just past the prefix that gbt_var_node_cp_len computes. For text, varchar and bpchar under a
multibyte encoding, 19rc1's gbt_var_node_cp_len ends that prefix at a character boundary, so the bytes compared are the
next characters' leading bytes, which whole ranges of characters share: C2 or C3 for all of Latin-1 Supplement, E4 to E9
for the CJK Unified Ideographs. Such a key then costs about the same in every subtree, gistchoose places it with no
regard to its order, and a point lookup descends into many subtrees. Reproduction (19rc1 14 buffers, patched 3):

	CREATE EXTENSION btree_gist;
	CREATE TABLE t (k text COLLATE "C");
	CREATE INDEX ON t USING gist (k);
	INSERT INTO t SELECT chr(19968 + g / 1000 % 1000) || chr(19968 + g % 1000) FROM generate_series(0, 19999) AS g;
	VACUUM ANALYZE t;
	SET enable_seqscan = off; SET enable_bitmapscan = off;
	EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) SELECT 1 FROM t WHERE k = '上一';

This is upstream's change: master b82d69abf6 ("Remove btree_gist's useless logic for encoding-aware truncation", Tom
Lane, 2026-07-03), which deletes the character alignment as dead code (with gbtree_vinfo.eml, which only the alignment
read), so the penalty measures at the first differing byte. Its btree_utils_var.c and btree_utils_var.h hunks apply as
they are; the other four files differ only in context. Upstream committed it to master alone, so 19.0 and 14 to 18 keep
the alignment, and the RC2 and GA repins keep this patch; the repin to PostgreSQL 20 drops it. A penalty only decides
where entries go, so an index built with or without the patch stays valid.

